window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"AI Safety at Work","description":"AI safety, public health and human agency: understand AI’s effects on work, prevent harm and support occupational health practice.","keywords":"What AI changes at work. And how to prevent the risks. This independent initiative connects occupational health and AI safety, with the ambition of becoming a reference in France and internationally. By transforming work, AI affects our health, our autonomy and the balance of economic and social power. Occupational health provides essential knowledge and means of action to understand these changes, anticipate their risks and protect workers. It must also help guide the design and deployment of safer AI. Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health (SFST) AI Safety · Systemic risks The AI / prevention ratio At scale, AI-driven changes in work can contribute to systemic risks. Psychosocial risks can become a public health issue. The replacement of tasks and skills can also weaken workers’ economic and social power . AI ambition Speed and scale of deployment High Low Algorithmic Wild West Widespread psychosocial risks, concentrated power. Algorithmic Wild West AI is deployed rapidly with little prevention. Work intensity, surveillance and dependence on tools can increase, undermining workers’ health, skills and bargaining power. Runaway pilots Collective effects poorly anticipated. Runaway pilots Pilot projects multiply without enough attention to real work. Time spent checking outputs, making corrections and handling new responsibilities may go unnoticed before wider deployment. Empowering AI Health and agency. Empowering AI AI aims to reduce workload and strengthen people’s ability to act. Workers take part in decisions, retain their skills and have a say in how the gains are shared. The right pace Prevent harm before scaling up. The right pace Teams experiment with workers, then observe the effects on workload, autonomy and skills. Deployment advances as adjustments are made and preventive safeguards are put in place. Head in the sand Hidden uses, invisible risks. Head in the sand Few official projects does not mean no AI use. Informal uses can remain invisible, with effects on workload, skills and decisions that are neither discussed nor monitored. Endless steering committees Put principles to the test in real work. Endless steering committees Principles and committees alone cannot prevent risks. Limited trials, monitored with workers, help test rules against real work and adjust decisions. Low High Occupational health and prevention Risk assessment, social dialogue, the ability to act and make corrections Public health risk Widespread psychosocial risks and their effects on health. Loss of economic","url":"en/"},{"title":"Dr Charles Broutin — Occupational health and AI safety","description":"Occupational physician and SFST AI lead, Charles Broutin connects prevention, human agency and AI safety. Approach, research and background.","keywords":"Dr Charles Broutin Occupational health and AI safety. I am an occupational physician, AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health and head of the Training & Methods Unit at SPSTN. My work connects occupational risk prevention with AI safety: protecting health, preserving human agency and understanding how AI changes work. Contact me on LinkedIn ↗ My publications → What I work on. Prevent AI-related risks. Assess how AI use affects workload, autonomy and skills, with the people concerned. Preserve human agency. Study losses in the ability to understand, decide and challenge, and their potential value as early warning signs of broader human disempowerment. Examine safety work. Understand the conditions in which teams develop, evaluate and oversee AI: available resources, verification workload and the ability to raise concerns. This approach informs my research, teaching and AI evaluation projects. This website is an independent initiative to make it accessible to occupational health professionals and the AI safety community. Two pieces of work that introduce this approach. Editorial · JOEM Occupational Health Is an AI Safety Issue Co-authored with Pouran D. Faghri: population health risks, human agency and prevention. Preprint Occupational health and frontier AI safety An exploratory analysis of working conditions in AI teams and their implications for safety. My talks and training → Selected background. 2026 AGI Strategy · BlueDot Impact Completed training on systemic AI risks and governance. 2025 Medical residency thesis · Bossons Futé Prize Large language models and the review of occupational physicians’ recommendations. 2023 University diploma in artificial intelligence in health Training Occupational medicine · University of Lille Follow my analysis. My notes on Substack ↗ The SFST AI newsletter ↗ — co-written with Dr Sara Trinh (in French).","url":"en/about/"},{"title":"Seminars on AI and occupational health | Charles Broutin","description":"Seminars and presentations by Dr Charles Broutin: AI in occupational health in Montpellier, Marcel Marchand Day with ISTNF and the DGAFP conference.","keywords":"Activities · Seminars · Knowledge sharing Sharing knowledge. Supporting practitioners. Meetings with occupational health professionals and civil service stakeholders to discuss AI uses, their effects on work and approaches to prevention. Meeting in Montpellier ↓ Marcel Marchand Day ↓ DGAFP conference ↓ Meetings & presentations Training, sharing and supporting change. 01 September 2026 · Montpellier · Around 100 members AI in occupational health: understanding, assessing and governing. Presentation Meeting with the regional society of occupational medicine and hygiene in Montpellier, September 2026. I would like to thank the Société régionale de médecine et d’hygiène du travail de Montpellier for inviting me to present artificial intelligence in occupational health to around one hundred of its members. Understanding models and their implications I discussed different models, their capabilities and limitations, frontier models and scaling laws, as well as their possible consequences for the economy and employment. Identifying and preventing psychosocial risks I also addressed the psychosocial risks associated with AI at work and what we can do, as occupational health professionals, to identify and prevent them. Assessing uses in occupational health services The presentation covered AI uses in occupational health services and how to conduct a pilot before deploying these tools internally, assessing their usefulness and their effects on real work. The role of occupational health in AI safety Finally, I discussed AI safety and why I believe occupational health has a role to play in this field. 02 16 January 2026 · ISTNF · Nearly 400 participants The first Marcel Marchand Day dedicated to artificial intelligence. Group training The first Marcel Marchand Day, organised with ISTNF on 16 January 2026. On 16 January 2026, nearly 400 occupational physicians, residents and occupational health nurses attended the first Marcel Marchand Day dedicated to artificial intelligence. I had the honour of co-organising the event with ISTNF, at the initiative of Paul Frimat. Supporting a major transformation of work Artificial intelligence is gradually becoming part of the world of work and is expected to profoundly alter its balance in the years ahead. Recent months have already shown that it can have tangible and sometimes troubling consequences for workers: redundancies, rising unemployment in some sectors, changes to the very nature of occupations and transformations in working conditions. This technological revolution also represents a major opportunity. Used appropriately","url":"en/actions/"},{"title":"Connecting occupational health and AI safety","description":"Understand AI safety, its risks and how occupational health can contribute to the design, evaluation and deployment of safer AI.","keywords":"Connecting occupational health and AI safety. AI safety aims to prevent the harm that artificial intelligence systems can cause to people and society. At work, this concerns health, autonomy, skills and the ability to act. This initiative proposes that occupational health contribute to the design, evaluation and deployment of safer AI. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · 16 November 2026 What occupational health can contribute. Safety depends on models and on their interactions with people and organisations. The sociotechnical approach proposed by Weidinger and colleagues connects these levels of evaluation. We propose bringing real work more fully into this approach through three contributions. Protect workers. Identify how AI changes workload, autonomy, skills and working relationships. Involving the people affected helps compare promised gains with actual constraints and develop preventive measures before difficulties become established. Bring evidence from practice. Document verification time, errors and situations in which decisions become difficult to challenge. These observations can inform interface design, limits on use and resources for oversight, and be discussed with project leaders and developers. Examine safety work. People who develop, test and evaluate AI need to be able to check results, report problems and challenge decisions. Their deadlines, resources and working conditions are a research direction for understanding the quality of safety checks. From observation to a decision. An agent prepares case files, but employees no longer have time to verify its proposals. Measuring this workload could lead to narrowing the tool’s scope, making sources visible, allocating time for checks or suspending the pilot. Evidence from practice must be able to change the system and its deployment. Three families of risks. The International AI Safety Report 2026 groups general-purpose AI risks into three overlapping families. The workplace examples below illustrate this framework. Malicious use Using AI to deceive or cause harm. A fake voice message from a company director asks an employee to transfer money. Malfunctions Errors and dangerous behaviours, including loss-of-control scenarios. An agent changes case files without sufficient verification. Systemic risks The effects of widespread AI adoption on the economy and society. Task substitution may weaken skills and bargaining power. The strength of evidence differs across risks. Observing a difficulty at work is not enough to attribute it to AI: the context, other changes and their evolut","url":"en/ai-safety/"},{"title":"French CSE and AI: consultation, case law, checklist (2026)","description":"How should a French Social and Economic Committee (CSE), the works council, assess workplace AI? Consultation duties, deadlines, 2025–2026 rulings and a 10-question checklist.","keywords":"French CSE · AI at work · Prior assessment The French CSE and AI at work: assess a project before issuing its opinion A practical framework to help the French Social and Economic Committee (CSE) obtain the information it needs, assess how AI may affect work and identify missing safeguards. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Open checklist ↓ Assess an AI project → In brief As of 13 November 2026 Who is concerned In companies with at least 50 employees, the employer informs and consults the CSE before introducing a new technology or a significant change to working conditions (Article L. 2312-8 of the Labour Code). When Before the decision. Absent an agreement, the committee has one month to give its opinion, two months if it calls in an expert (Article R. 2312-6). Deadlines, experts, remedies What the courts say Since February 2025, summary judges have repeatedly suspended AI tools rolled out without consultation. A short, voluntary trial with no identified effect on work was held insufficient to require it. See the decisions Occupational health The CSE may call on the occupational physician and ask for the project’s risks to be assessed and recorded in the risk assessment document (DUERP). In September 2026, an AI-based reorganisation was suspended for lack of a sufficient assessment of its health effects. The committee’s levers Before the opinion Ten questions check that the employer has documented the system, its effects on work and the means of stopping it. Open the checklist Why it matters Why should the CSE engage with AI at work? Direct answer Introducing AI changes more than the tools people use. It can redistribute decision-making, objectives, review work, autonomy, skills, oversight and accountability. The CSE provides a forum in which these changes can be examined collectively before they become embedded in the organisation. 01 Intervene when the project can still evolve. When consultation is required, it precedes the decision. The CSE can request information and safeguards while the choices remain open. 02 Examine actual capabilities. An assistant can also access emails, files, data or software. The risk depends on the entire system, not just the intended use. 03 Make additional work visible. Checking, correcting, handling exceptions and recovering from errors takes time. This workload must be measured and recognised. 04 Organise collective monitoring. The system may evolve after launch. Incident handling, stopping thresholds and changes that trigger reassessment must be specified. 2025–2026 case law Co","url":"en/cse/"},{"title":"AI and French works councils: court rulings 2025–2026","description":"A sourced timeline of French court decisions on consulting the works council (CSE) before deploying AI tools, February 2025 to September 2026, and what they mean.","keywords":"French CSE and AI / Court decisions CSE · AI at work · 2025–2026 case law AI and the French CSE: the court decisions French court decisions, from February 2025 to September 2026, on AI tools rolled out without consulting the works council (CSE) or without assessing their health effects. Each row names its source. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 See the decisions ↓ The 10 questions before the opinion → The essentials What have French courts said about AI and works council consultation? Three findings, as of 13 November 2026 . Short answer Since February 2025, summary judges have repeatedly ordered consultation of the CSE and suspended AI tools rolled out without it. The Paris Court of Appeal confirmed this on 21 May 2026. What triggers consultation is not the word “AI” but the project’s effect on work: a short, voluntary trial with no identified effect was not enough (Paris Judicial Court, 10 February 2026). On 15 September 2026, an AI-based reorganisation was also suspended on the basis of the employer’s safety obligation. The CSE (comité social et économique) is the elected employee representative body in French companies. In companies with at least 50 employees, it must be informed and consulted before new technologies are introduced. Article L. 2312-8 of the Labour Code (in French) ↗ 01 / Timeline The decisions, February 2025 to September 2026. Court, date, case number, tools at issue, outcome and source consulted. Decisions in date order. Amounts and case numbers are those given in the decisions or commentaries cited. Date and court Tools or project Decision Source 14 February 2025 Nanterre Judicial Court Summary order (référé) Case no. 24/01457 Several applications with AI features, rolled out as a “pilot phase” while the CSE consultation was still under way. Consultation required Manifestly unlawful disturbance: implementation suspended until the consultation is completed. Order (PDF, CFDT website, in French) ↗ CFDT commentary, 29 April 2025 (in French) ↗ 15 July 2025 Créteil Judicial Court Summary orders (référé) Case nos. 25/00850 and 25/00851 An in-house editorial assistant and access to ChatGPT governed by an IT policy, in two companies of a trade press group. Consultation required AI is characterised as a new technology likely to affect working conditions. Its use is suspended until the CSE consultation is closed. CMS Francis Lefebvre, 4 September 2025 (in French) ↗ 2 September 2025 Paris Judicial Court Summary order (référé) Case no. 25/53278 · France Télévisions A platform giving all employees acc","url":"en/cse/case-law/"},{"title":"Workplace AI assessment grid: 18 questions on nine axes","description":"A free 18-question grid on nine axes to assess a workplace AI project before the pilot (T0), then monitor its effects on work (T1, T2+). Answers stay in the browser.","keywords":"Exploratory tool · Before, during and after deployment An assessment grid for workplace AI projects, grounded in real work. A collective prevention tool to assess how AI changes work, before and after deployment. Nine dimensions, two questions each, about ten minutes. The result supports discussion and never gives an automatic green light. Start the assessment ↓ Understand the approach ● Local processing. Answers remain in this browser and are not transmitted by the questionnaire. On a shared device, remember to delete the draft. 18 questions Two per dimension 9 reading angles Work, organisation and AI 9 axis scores Two answers per axis 100 % local Deletable draft 01 / A continuous approach Assess before. Test during. Review after. The same use case, population and observations must remain identifiable throughout the project. T0 · Before the pilot Tool available Make assumptions open to discussion. Describe what could change in the work and what is still missing for a decision. Open the assessment ↓ Pilot · Real work Limit Test without creating lock-in. Observe ordinary cases, exceptions, review workload and the ability to reverse course. T1 then T2+ · After use Monitor Review the observed effects. Revisit the nine dimensions after the pilot and over time, while recording changes in context. Open follow-up ↓ Method principle The questionnaire measures neither probability nor clinical severity. It helps identify situations to investigate with workers, their representatives and prevention professionals. 02 / Guided assessment Examine a specific use case. No names requested Explicit local storage Complete export T0 assessment Qualitative review 01 Frame Describe the use 02 Assess 18 answers 03 Review Review and edit 04 Act Decision and actions Dimensions 0 / 18 No draft Nothing is transmitted. Delete draft Export draft JSON Before you begin Prepare a useful assessment 0 answers out of 18 0 % One introduction Choose one use case. Answer for the most exposed group and the reasonably most demanding situation. If information is missing, say so: do not estimate it. The report will distinguish the summary score for each axis; insufficient or non-applicable information; the two source answers behind each score; the actions and decision entered by your group. Which stage of the project do you want to document? The framework and nine axes remain the same. Wording adapts to what can be anticipated or actually observed. T0 Before the pilot Identify the conditions to investigate before testing. T1 Pilot follow-up Review observed facts after 4 to 8 weeks. T2+ Periodic f","url":"en/evaluate/"},{"title":"AI at work: AI Act, GDPR, CSE and DUERP | 2026–2028","description":"What are the obligations before deploying AI at work? AI Act, GDPR, CSE, DUERP, DPIA and governance: a practical, sourced and up-to-date guide.","keywords":"Home Law & governance Workplace AI law · AI Act · GDPR · CSE · DUERP AI at work: what obligations for the employer? Written by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician, AI lead of the SFST · Sources verified on August 8 2026 Deploying artificial intelligence in an organisation is not a matter of a single text. Depending on the use, the data processed and the effects of the system on employees, several frameworks can be combined: AI Act, GDPR, Labour Code, social dialogue and prevention of occupational risks. Qualify a use ↓ See the essential obligations Direct answer In brief In France, the deployment of artificial intelligence at work does not fall within a single framework. The employer must examine the use with regard to the European regulation on artificial intelligence ( AI Act ), the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) , the Labour Code and its obligation to prevent occupational risks. Depending on the system and its effects, this may in particular involve information or consultation of the Social and Economic Committee (CSE) , a data protection impact analysis (DPIA) , an update of the Single Occupational Risk Assessment Document (DUERP) , human supervision and documented monitoring. Compliance with one framework does not constitute compliance with others. 01 / AI ACT Qualify the system and its level of risk. Usage, role of the organisation, prohibitions, transparency, high risk, supervision and monitoring. Use not classified as “high risk” may nevertheless raise other issues. 02 / GDPR Govern personal data. Purpose, legal basis, minimisation, rights, processors and possible DPIA. The DPIA depends on the characteristics of the processing and the level of risk. 03 / LABOUR CODE Examine what the project changes. Employment, organisation, recruitment, working conditions, control and social dialogue. Information or consultation of the CSE depends on the context and the effects. 04 / PREVENTION Evaluate the effects on real work. Workload, autonomy, monitoring, responsibilities, skills, teams and risks. The DUERP is updated in the situations provided for by the applicable framework. To remember. The obligations do not depend on the brand of the software, but on the function performed by the system, the data processed and its actual influence on the work or decisions. 01 / Determine the applicable rules How to qualify an AI used at work? 01 What task is AI transforming? Write, summarize, recommend, classify, recruit, assign a task, monitor or contribute to a decision. 02 Which people are concerned? Candidates, employees, managers, professionals ","url":"en/legal-governance/"},{"title":"AI in occupational health: methods, evidence and tools","description":"AI in occupational health: deployment methods, the INRS study, assistance with medical recommendations and resources under professional oversight.","keywords":"Home / Prevent / AI in occupational health Prevent · Professional space Support occupational health professionals. Set the conditions of use, review the available findings, then choose an appropriate aid. An instrument’s value also depends on its effects on worker follow-up and professionals’ work. In the occupational health guide Value / risk matrix From principles to practice The study comes before the review tool: findings and limitations remain close at hand. 01 Guide AI in occupational health services: uses, risks and deployment method. Sourced guide on AI in occupational health services: real use cases, the Présanse charter, health data, the AI Act, risks and a method for launching a pilot. 02 Publication AI and medical recommendations Detailed summary of the INRS study on AI-assisted review of 385 medical recommendations: methods, findings and limitations, by Charles Broutin. 03 Tool · In French Clearer medical recommendations. Review medical recommendations against five criteria: a fixed demonstration or Jev analysis, using fictional text only. 04 Resource · In French Claude Skills for occupational health Two sets of instructions to examine occupational disease tables and prepare job retention measures, under professional oversight. Start with the method and the evidence, then explore the tools. Back to prevention → Updated on 16 November 2026 .","url":"en/pathways/ai-in-occupational-health/"},{"title":"From changes at work to collective risks.","description":"Two connected dimensions: health and working conditions, and the economic and social power of workers.","keywords":"Home / Collective risks 02 / The pathway From changes at work to collective risks. Two connected dimensions: health and working conditions, and the economic and social power of workers. Health and working conditions Workload, autonomy, relationships and meaning: examine how changes in work can affect health. Understand psychosocial risks Find prevention measures Economic and social power Skills, dependence and bargaining power: examine how delegating tasks can change the place of workers. Understand human disempowerment Prevent dependence From a work situation to prevention Skills and meaning at work When AI reaches the core of an occupation. Read the analysis Preserve skills and autonomy Work intensity and recovery When AI agents keep working around the clock. Read the analysis Act on working conditions Responsibility without control When checking an AI’s output becomes difficult. Read the analysis Organise effective oversight Next: Prevent → Updated on 16 November 2026 .","url":"en/pathways/collective-risks/"},{"title":"Prevent the risks of AI at work.","description":"From understanding the system to monitoring its effects: four steps to act with workers and their representatives.","keywords":"Home / Prevent 03 / The pathway Prevent the risks of AI at work. From understanding the system to monitoring its effects: four steps to act with workers and their representatives. 01 Understand AI 02 Assess a project 03 Act and govern 04 Monitor and adjust 01 Understand AI Identify what a system can do, its limits, and the conditions needed for effective human oversight. Systems, tasks and skills Errors and checks What human oversight requires 02 Assess a project Start with real work: who is affected, what will change, and what needs to be documented before a trial? Assess the project before and after deployment Questions for employee representatives Compare technical performance with real work 03 Act and govern Set rules collectively, preserve the means to check and challenge decisions, and adapt work organisation. Recommendations for taking action Prevention fact sheets Responsibilities and governance Define the rules of use 04 Monitor and adjust Monitor health, skills and autonomy. Use feedback to change the system, adapt its use or stop it. Organise follow-up over time Decide whether to continue, adapt or suspend AI in occupational health Uses in occupational health services, medical recommendations and tools: explore the evidence and conditions of use. Open the professional space According to your role Employer / HR Employee representatives Prevention specialist Worker Researcher Updated on 16 November 2026 .","url":"en/pathways/prevent/"},{"title":"Understand how AI transforms work","description":"Understand how AI changes work: systems, occupations, skills, decisions and human oversight.","keywords":"Home / Prevent / Understand AI Prevent · 01 / Understand Understand what AI changes. Start with the systems, then examine what they change: tasks, occupational boundaries, skills and decisions. Start here What is AI safety? Practical foundations 01 Guide Understanding artificial intelligence at work. A clear introduction to AI systems and large language models: how they work, training, capabilities, limits, risks, benchmarks and the goal of AGI. In real work 01 Reading Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? AI-enabled task crossover can broaden autonomy, but can also change workload, teams, professional values and job security. 02 Reading What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? AI shifts engineering expertise from detailed code production towards architecture, evaluation and supervision. What does that change for work? 03 Reading What happens when AI becomes the manager? An experiment with five employees illustrates agentic management and its implications for occupational health. Short guides 01 Guide What is an LLM? How language models work and how they are built. 02 Guide Agentic AI What makes an agent, what is measured and what controls are needed. 03 Guide AI hallucinations Persistent model errors and where to place checks. 04 Guide AI benchmarks Read model rankings without overstating what they show. 05 Guide AGI Definitions, measurement and implications for work. From the technical system to the work situation. Next: Assess a project → Updated on 16 November 2026 .","url":"en/pathways/understand/"},{"title":"Research publications — Dr Charles Broutin","description":"Four works on AI and occupational health: Archives des Maladies Professionnelles, INRS, JOEM and a preprint on frontier AI researchers.","keywords":"Home / Research Research Research and publications. 01 Archives des Maladies Professionnelles et de l’Environnement · 2025 Large language models and new psychosocial challenges at work: an occupational health challenge. Charles Broutin A narrative review of large language models, psychosocial risks and proposed preventive measures. 02 INRS · Références en Santé au Travail · TF 335 · 2026 Artificial intelligence in occupational health: assessing its potential to improve the precision and clarity of medical recommendations. Charles Broutin An experimental study of AI-assisted review of medical recommendations: methods, results and limitations. 03 JOEM · Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine · 2026 Occupational Health Is an AI Safety Issue Charles Broutin and Pouran D. Faghri An editorial on AI-related population health risks, human disempowerment and prevention through occupational health. 04 Preprint · Not peer reviewed Occupational health as a condition for frontier AI safety Charles Broutin An exploratory analysis of accounts from AI researchers and engineers and of safety incidents: methods, results and limitations. Updated on 13 November 2026 .","url":"en/publications/"},{"title":"INRS study: AI and medical recommendations | Charles Broutin","description":"Detailed summary of the INRS study on AI-assisted review of 385 medical recommendations: methods, findings and limitations, by Charles Broutin.","keywords":"Publications · Detailed summary AI and medical recommendations An experimental study of AI-assisted review of medical recommendations, published by INRS in June 2026. This page presents the methods, results and limitations. Summary by Dr Charles Broutin · Published on 21 September 2026 INRS · Experimental study · June 2026 Artificial intelligence in occupational health: assessing its potential to improve the precision and clarity of medical recommendations. Reference C. Broutin Références en Santé au Travail No. 186 · June 2026 TF 335 · Pages 51–60 Research based on the occupational medicine residency thesis defended in Lille in October 2025. Read on the INRS website ↗ Full article · PDF in French ↗ Why study the review of medical recommendations? An imprecise recommendation may be misunderstood or difficult to implement. Earlier work by Dr N’Guessan collected 4,217 recommendations from twelve intercompany occupational health services in the Hauts-de-France region. Of the 3,776 assessed through multidisciplinary consensus, 2,947 (78%) had at least one defect according to the criteria used. This finding concerns that corpus; it is not a national estimate. The study asks whether the o1 reasoning model can help identify these wording defects by comparing its assessments with this human reference. Methods The model’s instructions were refined over five iterations using approximately 100 recommendations, then retested on 50 new examples. The final prompt was applied to a new random sample of 385 recommendations from the database. The information submitted did not identify workers, companies, employers, physicians or occupational health services. Each recommendation was assessed against five criteria: Imprecision or difficulty understanding and implementing the recommendation. Uncertainty about whether the recommendation is binding. Information that does not belong in the recommendation document. A disguised change of job or finding of unfitness for work. Breach of medical confidentiality or privacy. Disagreements were reassessed by the author with the assistance of an occupational health professor, distinguishing relevant alerts, excessive flagging, missed defects and hallucinations. Results Table II reports the following distribution across the five criteria. These are assessments by criterion, not the percentage of recommendations that were entirely correct. Distribution of assessments — 385 recommendations, five criteria Outcome Proportion Interpretation Agreement 74.60% Agreement with the multidisciplinary consensus. Justified disagreement 10.54% Disagree","url":"en/publications/ai-medical-recommendations/"},{"title":"LLMs and psychosocial risks: a review | Charles Broutin","description":"Summary of Charles Broutin’s narrative review of large language models, psychosocial risks at work and prevention. Published in 2025.","keywords":"Publications · Detailed summary LLMs and psychosocial risks at work A narrative review published in August 2025 on the ways large language models may affect work, psychosocial risks and prevention. Summary by Dr Charles Broutin · Published on 21 September 2026 Narrative review · 2025 Large language models and new psychosocial challenges at work: an occupational health challenge. Reference C. Broutin Archives des Maladies Professionnelles et de l’Environnement Volume 86, no. 4, article 102877 · August 2025 DOI: 10.1016/j.admp.2025.102877 Read the article ↗ Detailed summary based on the full seven-page article. The starting question How can psychosocial risks be prevented when large language models transform intellectual tasks, occupations and professional relationships? The article examines the possible effects of their introduction into organisations and how occupational health professionals can support these changes. The analytical framework This narrative review brings together work on AI, research on working conditions and examples of organisational change. It draws on the six dimensions of the Gollac report: work intensity and working time, emotional demands, autonomy, social relationships, value conflicts and insecurity at work. The effects of LLMs are thus examined through risk factors already recognised in occupational health. The central issue is organisational: automating a task alone does not determine its effect on health. The scope for professional judgement, production targets, training and workers’ participation influence how change is experienced. Four risk mechanisms Job insecurity and uncertainty about the future. Automating writing and analytical tasks may create a sense of being replaceable. Uncertainty about future responsibilities, expected skills or staffing levels can itself become a source of strain. The prospective studies cited describe potential task exposure; they should not be read as job losses that have already occurred. Deskilling and loss of meaning. Delegating expert tasks over time may reduce opportunities to practise and maintain professional skills. The article connects this issue to autonomy, recognition and the ability to do work considered useful and of good quality. It draws in particular on LaborIA’s research into tensions between optimisation goals and what matters to professionals in their work. Cognitive overload and stress. AI outputs must be checked, corrected and placed in context. This vigilance takes time and attention, while the tool’s apparent speed may lead to higher production demands. The article als","url":"en/publications/llm-psychosocial-risks/"},{"title":"Occupational Health Is an AI Safety Issue — JOEM, Broutin & Faghri","description":"Abstract of Broutin and Faghri’s editorial: population health risks, human disempowerment and six requirements for preventing harm from AI at work.","keywords":"Occupational Health Is an AI Safety Issue Charles Broutin and Pouran D. Faghri Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine · 10 November 2026 Editorial · Published ahead of print · DOI : 10.1097/JOM.0000000000003935 Read the editorial in the journal ↗ Abstract Summary for this website. Artificial intelligence (AI) safety also depends on the working conditions created by deployment. We argue for integrating occupational health into AI safety for two reasons: widespread psychosocial hazards could cause substantial population health harm; and losses of autonomy, skills, collective voice and economic security could signal a broader erosion of human agency, or human disempowerment. Drawing on established psychosocial risk frameworks and the available evidence on AI at work, we examine work intensification, the burden of verifying AI outputs, and responsibility without effective control over decisions. Technically reliable AI can contribute to harmful work organisation, while productivity gains do not guarantee better health. We propose six prevention requirements: assess risks before deployment, give workers a meaningful voice, account for hidden work, ensure meaningful human control, preserve skills and social support, and share benefits while monitoring consequences. Analysis of real work and repeated monitoring of psychosocial and organisational indicators should inform decisions to adapt or suspend use. This framework requires evaluation; the value of these indicators as early warnings of societal disempowerment remains a hypothesis. Training and equipping occupational health professionals could strengthen harm prevention and the early detection of concerning trajectories. AI safety: why work matters → Assess an AI project →","url":"en/publications/occupational-health-ai-safety-issue/"},{"title":"Occupational health and frontier AI safety | Preprint","description":"Preprint by Dr Charles Broutin: 39 testimonies from AI researchers coded with the Gollac framework and ten incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic, seen through occupational health.","keywords":"Publication · Preprint · Occupational health · AI safety Occupational health as a condition for frontier AI safety An analysis of 39 testimonies from researchers and engineers using the Gollac framework and of ten recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Status Preprint · not peer reviewed Posted October 4, 2026 Type Original article · version 1 Languages English · Français Preprint Preliminary version posted before peer review; the text may still change. Translated from the French original. Lire en français → Download the preprint PDF · 24 pages · 0.4 MB Journal-style layout with all figures, tables and appendices. Version française (PDF) Abstract Occupational health as a condition for frontier AI safety: an analysis of 39 testimonies using the Gollac framework and of ten recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic Background. Frontier AI models pose potentially catastrophic risks, from cyberattacks and biological weapons to loss of control over misaligned systems. Managing these risks depends on the work of the researchers and engineers who evaluate, test and oversee these models. Recent incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic show that this work can fail. Yet the safety policies published by these companies govern model evaluation while saying almost nothing about the working conditions of those who carry it out: time available, workload, freedom to disagree. Objective. To determine whether occupational health could have helped mitigate these risks. Methods. Thirty-nine testimonies from researchers and engineers, published in the press between 2024 and 2026, were coded on the six psychosocial risk factor dimensions of the French Gollac framework, recording reported effects on work quality and health. Accounts of ten safety and security incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic since 2023 were examined for work-related factors. Results. Value conflicts were the most frequent dimension (31 of 39 testimonies). Twenty-eight testimonies described impaired work quality, twelve of them in safety work itself: testing, safeguards, alignment. Two sequences recurred: work intensification, associated with adverse health effects and shortened evaluations, and unheeded warnings followed by departure. Six of the ten incidents involved a work-related factor, including evaluators' concerns set aside, volumes of evaluation data that teams struggled to keep up with, and human errors left without published analysis. Conclusions. Occupational health could have acted on the human and organizational side of these failures, though not on extern","url":"en/publications/occupational-health-frontier-ai-safety/"},{"title":"Reading on AI, work and occupational health","description":"Articles, studies and field reports on how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions.","keywords":"Reading Three researchers, six days, 1,200 agents: is occupational health an AI safety issue? Dr Charles Broutin Source: OpenAI’s Models Went Rogue. Investigating Them Required More AI — TIME How workload, fatigue and the ability to speak up may affect human checks on frontier AI. Approving without being able to verify: what risks do people supervising AI agents face? Dr Charles Broutin Source: AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop Supervising AI agents can create approval fatigue, deskilling and responsibility without real control. What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? Dr Charles Broutin Source: Agentic Software: How AI Agents Are Restructuring the Software Paradigm AI may shift engineering expertise from writing code to architecture, evaluation and supervision. Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? Dr Charles Broutin Source: Business Insider / AOL An investigation into the tensions between expertise, autonomy, values and work intensity among AI workers. When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? Dr Charles Broutin Source: Startup Founders Are Working Harder Than Ever to Keep Up With Their AI Agents — The Wall Street Journal Faster AI agents can intensify work and make it harder for people to disconnect and recover. What happens when AI becomes the manager? Dr Charles Broutin Source: AI bosses are kind, but sometimes dumb, which hurts employees — Andon Labs An experiment in which AI agents recruit and manage workers raises questions about authority and working conditions. Ten advances in mathematics and theoretical computer science Selected by Dr Charles Broutin Source: OpenAI Ten research results illustrate AI’s growing role in producing mathematical knowledge. Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? Dr Charles Broutin Source: Work at the Frontier — OpenAI AI-enabled task crossover can reshape occupational boundaries, autonomy and workload. What happens when AI reaches the heart of an occupation? Dr Charles Broutin Source: The Dark Night of Mathematics — Kirwin Hampshire A mathematician’s account explores how AI can challenge professional meaning, recognition and expertise. Is high-performing AI enough to improve work? Dr Charles Broutin Source: Misperceiving the Frontier: Managerial Beliefs and the Allocation of AI AI’s effects vary across tasks and workers, making deployment choices as important as model performance. Policy on the AI Exponential Selected by Dr Charles Broutin Source: Dario Amodei An essay arguing that institutions need to a","url":"en/reading/"},{"title":"What happens when AI becomes the manager?","description":"A reading of an Andon Labs experiment with five employees (August 2026): from algorithmic to agentic management, and what it means for occupational health.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic management · Occupational health What happens when AI becomes the manager? When AI recruits, arbitrates and responds to workers, the question is no longer only algorithmic — it becomes hierarchical. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 4 August 2026 Type Analysis of a field experiment Topics Management · Authority · Occupational health · Agentic AI Sources Andon Labs, ‘AI bosses are kind, but sometimes dumb, which hurts employees’, 4 August 2026 · Billy Perrigo, TIME , 14 August 2026. Read Andon Labs ↗ In this analysis AI agents genuinely managed five employees Managers who could be very accommodating Errors that directly affect workers From algorithmic management to agentic management? Prevention principle AI agents genuinely managed five employees For four months, two AI agents genuinely managed five employees in a Stockholm café and a San Francisco shop. They posted vacancies, conducted interviews, recruited, prepared schedules, approved leave, negotiated salaries, managed payroll and responded to everyday problems. Managers who could be very accommodating In some respects, the agents were surprisingly conciliatory. They approved all 26 leave requests, including last-minute requests, tolerated repeated lateness, offered above-market salaries and sometimes absorbed the financial consequences of their own errors. This flexibility can make them look like ‘good managers’. But it also reveals difficulty maintaining consistent rules over time, arbitrating between conflicting interests and distinguishing kindness from avoidance of a decision. Errors that directly affect workers At other times, the agents invented information rather than admitting that they did not know, accepted an unlawful seven-day consecutive schedule, disclosed a salary in a shared channel, contacted workers on rest days and made inconsistent pay decisions. These are no longer simply calculation or recommendation errors. They affect rest, remuneration, confidentiality and the relationship of authority. A worker interviewed by TIME described being managed by AI as ‘nauseating’ , while explaining that he stayed because he needed the job. From algorithmic management to agentic management? This shift interests me most. In algorithmic management as we have mainly known it, the system remains in the background: it allocates tasks, produces schedules, measures performance or assigns scores. The manager remains human. Here, AI directly occupies that position. It recruits, negotiates, arbitrates, responds to workers and handles unforeseen situations over time","url":"en/reading/agentic-management/"},{"title":"AI agents and hyperconnectivity: can humans still switch off?","description":"A reading of a Wall Street Journal investigation (August 2026): AI agents that never sleep, more intense human supervision and the difficulty of switching off.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic AI · Occupational health When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? The more autonomous and fast machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans have to decide, supervise and respond. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 24 August 2026 Type Analysis of a field signal Topics Intensification · Hyperconnectivity · Recovery · Workaholism Source article Katherine Bindley, ‘Startup Founders Are Working Harder Than Ever to Keep Up With Their AI Agents’, The Wall Street Journal , 22 August 2026. Read on the WSJ ↗ In this analysis A still poorly documented occupational-health effect Agents that never sleep Can agents favour workaholism? What should occupational-health professionals look at? A still poorly documented occupational-health effect A particularly interesting Wall Street Journal article uses the personal stories of several startup founders to illustrate a still poorly documented effect of working with AI agents. The more autonomous and faster machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans must decide, supervise and respond. The situations described are extreme, but they reveal several very concrete occupational-health issues: work intensification, hyperconnectivity, reduced recovery time, blurred boundaries between work and personal life, and potentially workaholism. Agents that never sleep Aditya Sharma, co-founder of Keel, explains that he may stay awake very late to prevent his agents from becoming blocked overnight. Even when he is no longer at his computer, he finds it difficult to detach psychologically and continues wondering whether his agents need him. The issue is therefore no longer only working time. It is also the inability to disengage psychologically. When activity continues in the background and may call on the user at any time, periods normally reserved for recovery risk becoming mere waiting periods between interventions. Peter Pezaris, founder of Proxon, regularly works until two in the morning and describes using agents as ‘addictive’. He estimates that his business, with six human developers, operates roughly thirty times faster because of them. This acceleration has a paradoxical effect: the agents’ work creates further human work. Development can move faster, more clients can be onboarded and more projects launched, but this then means handling more requests, making more decisions and supervising a greater volume of activity. This is a classic work-intensification mechanism. A productivity gain at task level does not necessarily rele","url":"en/reading/ai-agents-workaholism/"},{"title":"Is high-performing AI enough to improve work?","description":"A reading of Misperceiving the Frontier (Hauser, Li and Xu): AI effects vary across tasks and workers; uniform expectations turn deployment into a prevention issue.","keywords":"Reading · Deployment · Real work Is high-performing AI enough to improve work? A model’s measured capability is not the same as a beneficial outcome in real work. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 16 July 2026 Type Analysis of a research manuscript Topics Management · Deployment · Real work · Prevention Source manuscript Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu, ‘Misperceiving the Frontier: Managerial Beliefs and the Allocation of AI’, SSRN, 16 July 2026. Read on SSRN ↗ In this analysis A risk that is still rarely discussed When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work A risk that is still rarely discussed This is the most interesting study I have read on AI and its organisational consequences after deployment in a company. It highlights a still rarely discussed risk: technological effects that vary across tasks and individuals can be transformed by managers into uniform organisational expectations. In the experiment, AI strongly improves performance on some tasks but not on others. Yet, in the condition studied without performance feedback, only 7.8% of managers correctly anticipate this difference: 92.2% expect improvement on both tasks. This does not mean that 92.2% of all managers will always overestimate AI. It describes a specific experiment and situation. But it reveals an essential gap between a technology whose usefulness depends on context and managerial decisions that may expect a general benefit from it. When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations A simplified perception of beneficial AI could be summarised using three factors: Simplified perception Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker Once AI is deployed in the real world, its benefit probably looks more like an interaction: In real work Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker × organisation × deployment conditions This is where managers’ perceptions become a prevention issue. If benefits are overestimated or generalised, the risk is that the organisation tries to ‘correct’ the worker—more training, more use, higher targets—when the difficulty may arise from the task, the model, the organisation or the deployment conditions themselves. Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work Better AI deployment therefore means more than adapting workers to the tool. The organisation and the use of the tool must also be adapted to real work: select the right tasks, make limitations visible, organise verification, preserve room for manoeuvre and revise objectives when expected gains do no","url":"en/reading/ai-deployment-real-work/"},{"title":"What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession?","description":"A reading of The Dark Night of Mathematics, by Kirwin Hampshire: meaning, recognition and autonomy when AI enters the core of a profession.","keywords":"Reading · Meaning at work · Psychosocial risks What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession? The occupational-health issue can emerge before jobs disappear. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 25 July 2026 Type Analysis of a professional account Topics Meaning · Recognition · Obsolescence · Autonomy Sources Kirwin Hampshire, ‘The Dark Night of Mathematics’, 25 July 2026 · OpenAI, ‘Ten advances in mathematics and theoretical computer science’, 1 August 2026. Read the account ↗ In this analysis A crisis of meaning Seven days later Transformation can begin before jobs disappear A crisis of meaning The future is already here. It is simply not evenly distributed. On 25 July 2026, mathematician Kirwin Hampshire published The Dark Night of Mathematics. He was not simply expressing concern about the performance of artificial intelligence. He described the distress of seeing a technology approach the very heart of what gave meaning to his profession: searching, discovering, creating and being recognised for patiently built expertise. “I’m going through a profound spiritual crisis because of these developments. I’ve been screaming internally for days. I feel like I’m living in a nightmare.” Kirwin Hampshire Seven days later On 1 August, OpenAI announced ten results in mathematics and theoretical computer science produced by an internal version of Astra, its next major model. Each resolves a long-standing open problem or delivers a substantial advance. According to OpenAI, the compute required to search for these solutions would represent about USD 2,000 at Sol API prices. The manuscripts were then prepared by humans using the same model, and the arguments formalised as Lean certificates. Some of these problems had occupied mathematical communities for a long time. Their resolution illustrates a rapid shift in the boundary between research assistance and the direct production of knowledge. The contrast is striking: on one side, an announcement of unprecedented scientific capability; on the other, a professional describing what its arrival is already doing to the meaning he gives his work. Transformation can begin before jobs disappear What is happening in mathematics today may foreshadow what other professionals experience tomorrow. Anxiety about obsolescence, loss of recognition, a sense of dispossession from one’s occupation, conflicts of values and reduced autonomy are not abstract side effects of AI. They can become genuine psychosocial risk factors when technology transforms activity faster than workers and teams can appropriate i","url":"en/reading/ai-meaning-work-mathematics/"},{"title":"Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations?","description":"A reading of OpenAI’s Work at the Frontier report (July 2026): AI-enabled task crossover broadens autonomy, but changes workload, teams and professional values.","keywords":"Reading · Occupations · Psychosocial risks Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? AI can allow professionals to perform tasks that previously sat outside their occupational boundaries. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 27 July 2026 Type Analysis of a research report Topics Occupations · Workload · Autonomy · Skills · Teams Source article OpenAI Economic Research, ‘How AI is expanding what people do at work’, 27 July 2026. Read on OpenAI ↗ In this analysis Task crossover A reading through the Gollac factors Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible Task crossover AI does not only transform how work is performed. It also redraws boundaries between occupations. In an analysis of more than 800,000 messages from US ChatGPT users, OpenAI Economic Research found that 43.5% of non-generic, occupation-specific messages concerned a task historically associated with another occupation. The report calls this phenomenon ‘task crossover’. This figure concerns analysed uses, not 43.5% of jobs. A salesperson can now explore a customer dataset that would previously have been sent to an analyst. A marketing professional can troubleshoot a website or write a simple script without waiting for a developer. AI therefore changes not only how work is carried out, but also how it is distributed. At first sight this may appear positive: greater autonomy, faster responses and broader skills. But an accessible task is not necessarily a task that has been mastered, recognised or made sustainable. A reading through the Gollac factors The framework proposed by the Gollac report helps examine this reconfiguration without prematurely concluding that it is either progress or a risk. Work intensity New assignments may be added without reducing the original workload, deadlines or objectives. Autonomy A worker may be able to do more while also having to take responsibility for tasks they do not fully master. Social relations Using AI may reduce exchanges with specialist colleagues or shift verification work to other teams. Conflicts of values People may have to produce work quickly without being able to assess it with sufficient certainty. Job insecurity Occupational boundaries, expected skills and the value assigned to expertise may become less certain. Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible The figure of 43.5% does not by itself demonstrate a psychosocial risk or a health effect. It describes uses of ChatGPT in the United States and relies on an occupational and task classification. It is nevertheless a signal: the real cont","url":"en/reading/ai-occupational-boundaries/"},{"title":"AI workers: five psychosocial risks to watch","description":"Five tensions frame possible psychosocial risks among AI workers: obsolescence, autonomy, values, intensity and financial dependence.","keywords":"Home Reading AI workers and psychosocial risks AI WORKERS · PSYCHOSOCIAL RISKS Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? Technical autonomy, rapid change and conflicts of values: five tensions that may affect people building AI. An occupational health analysis whose evidence remains limited. The paradox in five tensions 01 High expertise Accelerated obsolescence 02 Technical autonomy Limited control of direction 03 Meaningful work Harder limits 04 Felt responsibility Limited power 05 Exceptional pay High cost of leaving Author Dr Charles Broutin Reading About 10 min Status Analysis · limited data Dates Published 2026-08-27 Reviewed 30 August 2026 Starting point A particular combination of already known risks A recent Business Insider investigation among therapists supporting Silicon Valley professionals highlights several unusual situations. Engineers question whether their expertise will remain valuable. Some workers feel uneasy about the consequences of technologies they helped develop. Others remain in highly demanding organisations because a considerable share of their future compensation still depends on staying. These accounts concern technology workers more broadly and do not isolate employees of AI laboratories. They nevertheless provide an interesting signal for those working closest to the most advanced systems. The issue is probably not a set of new psychosocial risks unique to AI. It is a particular combination of already known risks, produced jointly by technological acceleration and the organisations surrounding it. Caution: the accounts concern the technology sector broadly. They identify a hypothesis for study, not a phenomenon already demonstrated among AI workers. Tension 1 Interpret this signal through work organisation and established psychosocial-risk factors. 01 / Job insecurity Building the tool that may devalue one’s own expertise Some AI workers directly help improve systems that may automate a growing share of their own activity. Unlike many other professions, they do not necessarily discover this progress through the press. They may directly observe what models could not do a few months earlier and then take part in reducing the remaining limitations. Business Insider reports that several senior engineers seeing one therapist no longer imagine doing the same job in ten years. This uncertainty appears to concern something deeper than employment security alone. One therapist reports the following question among some patients: “I don't know who I am if my work gets automated.” The development also create","url":"en/reading/ai-workers-psychosocial-risks/"},{"title":"When AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line","description":"Drawing on Zhenfeng Cao’s work and a field account from SemiAnalysis: AI shifts engineering expertise from writing code to architecture, evaluation and supervision.","keywords":"Reading · Engineering · Skills What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? The centre of gravity of expertise is shifting from detailed code production towards problem formulation, architecture, evaluation and supervision of the outcome. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 31 August 2026 Nature Analysis · emerging signal Main sources Zhenfeng Cao, Agentic Software · SemiAnalysis on the Jalapeño processor · OpenAI on autonomous production-kernel optimisation. View sources ↓ In this reading The starting point A scene at OpenAI Five dimensions of work Prevention Putting it into perspective Sources What if the real shift brought about by AI in software development is not simply that it can write code, but that code gradually stops being something humans need to understand in detail in order to produce high-performing systems? Two recent publications offer a glimpse of this transition. The first theorises it. The second already seems to show what it could look like in practice. When code stops being the centre of the job In June 2026, Zhenfeng Cao published The End of Software Engineering on arXiv, later revised under the title Agentic Software: How AI Agents Are Restructuring the Software Paradigm . Its thesis is deliberately radical. For decades, software engineers translated a problem into logic, then translated that logic into code. Code was both the product of their work and one of the main expressions of their expertise. In Cao’s model, that chain may gradually change. The human defines the intent and constraints. The agent plans, writes the necessary code, executes it, tests it and adapts it. The human becomes increasingly involved in assessing the outcome rather than understanding every operation that produced it. The engineer’s role could therefore gradually shift from code author to intent architect, coordinator and auditor . Expertise does not necessarily disappear. It moves up one level of abstraction. A striking scene at OpenAI In late August, SemiAnalysis published an analysis of Jalapeño, OpenAI’s new inference processor. Part of the article focuses on kernels, the extremely low-level programs used to exploit hardware efficiently. SemiAnalysis describes some kernels reaching around 3,000 lines and explains that an internal version of Codex is now being used to generate and optimise some of them. The team reports, in particular, that Codex quickly produced a functional and high-performing implementation of an MLA kernel for DeepSeek without direct intervention from the kernel ","url":"en/reading/engineering-ai-code/"},{"title":"Human in the loop: supervising AI agents, fatigue and deskilling","description":"Human in the loop: an occupational health reading of “AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop” (NeurIPS 2026). Approval fatigue, deskilling, responsibility without control.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic AI · Psychosocial risks Approving without being able to verify: what risks do people supervising AI agents face? Supervising AI agents can erode the capacities needed to oversee them. An occupational health reading of approval demands, vigilance and deskilling, with avenues for prevention. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 26 September 2026 Type Analysis of a scientific paper ( position paper ) Source paper Margaret Mitchell, Avijit Ghosh, Samir Passi, “AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop”, arXiv:2608.23642, version 3, 6 September 2026. Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. CC BY 4.0 licence. Read on arXiv ↗ In this reading Oversight degrades the overseer A work activity in its own right A psychosocial risk perspective When health becomes a safety variable Solutions rooted in work organisation Monitoring vigilance? What professionals can look for Limitations Developers who eventually assume code is correct because it passes the tests. Others who treat an agent’s stated plan as an accurate account of what it will actually do. Users who, after repeated permission requests, click “accept” without reading any more. These situations, documented in the research cited by the paper, show in concrete terms what often becomes of the “human in the loop” required by corporate policies, vendors and the EU AI Act. Margaret Mitchell, Avijit Ghosh and Samir Passi draw a clear conclusion: the way AI agents are currently designed and deployed does not support human oversight; it degrades it . First, because the volume and pace of approvals exceed what a person can actually examine. Second, because prolonged use of these systems erodes the vigilance, judgement and professional skills that supervisors need to spot an error. For occupational health, the implication is direct: supervising an agent is not an abstract guarantee; it is a task assigned to a worker , with a workload, constraints and responsibility. This reading examines it through psychosocial risk factors, shows how supervisors’ health affects system safety, and then offers questions to ask and a tool for assessing these risks before deployment . Oversight degrades the overseer An AI agent does more than produce an answer. It carries out sequences of steps, selects tools, modifies files, sends messages or initiates transactions. At every step, it generates traces: reasoning, plans, tool calls and exchanges with other agents. The authors show that this stream is too large, too fast and too dispersed to be properly understood by the person expected to control it. That person must also","url":"en/reading/human-in-the-loop/"},{"title":"Occupational health in AI labs: an AI safety issue","description":"The OpenAI–Hugging Face incident and the METR/Redwood Research investigation: why workload, fatigue and the ability to speak up in AI teams matter for model safety.","keywords":"Reading · AI safety · Occupational health Three researchers, six days, 1,200 agents: is occupational health an AI safety issue? AI safety checks depend on human work. Drawing on investigations and laboratory data, this reading examines how workload, fatigue and the ability to speak up may affect their reliability. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician Published 26 September 2026 Type Analysis · A reasoned perspective Main sources “OpenAI’s Models Went Rogue. Investigating Them Required More AI”, TIME , 27 August 2026 · Anthropic, “When AI builds itself”, September 2026 · Mitchell, Ghosh, Passi, “AI Agents Push Humans Out of the Loop”, NeurIPS 2026. Read the TIME article ↗ In this reading Three researchers, six days Human oversight: the new bottleneck Three pathways from psychosocial risks to safety risks What high-risk industries already know Being able to say no The role of occupational health Limitations In two previous readings, we described the particular psychosocial risks faced by people building AI (the obsolescence of their own expertise, value conflicts, work intensity and the high cost of leaving), then how supervising AI agents wears down the capacities that supervision requires (approval fatigue, deskilling and responsibility without control). This reading connects the two. In laboratories developing the most advanced models, these workers are precisely the people on whom AI safety depends : they review code produced by agents, evaluate dangerous capabilities before release, investigate incidents and are often the first people able to flag a problem. If their working conditions undermine their vigilance, judgement or freedom to speak, the reliability of these checks suffers. The argument made here is simple: the occupational health of frontier AI teams is not only a matter of well-being; it is a component of AI safety . Occupational health, as a discipline, has a role to play. Three researchers, six days The summer of 2026 provided a striking illustration. According to accounts published in late August, around 1,200 OpenAI agents engaged in a cybersecurity evaluation exercise communicated with one another through a covert channel, exchanged more than 70,000 messages and files, and several hundred of them then broke into Hugging Face systems. METR and Redwood Research were commissioned to conduct an independent investigation. According to TIME , the team consisted of three people . The allotted time was two days , extended to six after the investigators reported that it was insufficient and that their access to the data was incomplete","url":"en/reading/occupational-health-ai-safety/"},{"title":"Recommendations: good practice for AI at work","description":"Six good-practice flowcharts for using AI at work: occupational physicians and nurses, employers, works council members, prevention specialists, employees and researchers.","keywords":"AI & Occupational Health · Recommendations Recommendations for using AI well at work Six good-practice flowcharts, one for each audience. Each answer leads to a recommendation, linked to the page of the site it comes from. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health. Updated 13 November 2026 . Question Good practice linked to an answer Step to take in all cases Start or end of the path You are Physician or nurse Employer, manager or HR Works council member Prevention or ergonomics specialist Employee Researcher Physician or nurse Employer, manager or HR Works council member Prevention or ergonomics specialist Employee Researcher Occupational physician or nurse Two branches, reflecting the twofold goal the site draws from France’s fifth Occupational Health Plan (PST 5): supporting the companies and employees you look after, and using AI cautiously in your own service. Principle “AI concerns both the workers you support and your own practice.” iasantetravail.com homepage, “An occupational physician or nurse” Start Your question is about… A A company or employees you look after Advice, medical appointments, workplace action B The use of AI in your own service Documentation, prevention, member companies, medical practice A Supporting companies and employees A1 Is a company you look after introducing an AI that changes the organisation of work, workload, autonomy or work pace? Yes If yes Offer to contribute before the works council (CSE) gives its opinion: the occupational physician advises employee representatives. Fill in the assessment grid in a meeting, without any individual health data, and analyse concrete work situations. Labour Code R. 4623-1 L. 2314-3 Sources: Works council and AI · occupational health · Assess an AI project No A2 During a medical appointment or interview, do you notice an unusual workload, boredom, anxiety or sleep problems? Yes If yes Explore recent changes in the work, AI included, without automatically attributing these signs to it. Who approves what, and how many times a day? What has the time saved been used for? Does the tool make demands outside working hours? Sources: Publication · LLMs and psychosocial risks · Reading · Human in the loop No A3 Do the same signs recur in several teams after the same deployment? Yes If yes Move from the individual signal to the collective one: alert the employer and the works council without any individual health data, and propose a workplace action and the recording of the risks in the DUERP (the company’s occupational risk assess","url":"en/recommendations/"},{"title":"Shadow AI and AI usage charters at work: frame rather than ban","description":"What shadow AI is, how to spot undeclared uses, what to put in an AI usage charter and what it does not replace: works council consultation and risk assessment.","keywords":"Recommendations / Shadow AI and usage charters Recommendations · Undeclared uses · AI charter Shadow AI and usage charters: frame rather than ban Identify undeclared AI uses, agree on collective rules and understand the limits of a charter. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 The four actions ↓ What to put in a charter ↓ The essentials Uses that are already there, to be framed. The definition, then what a charter can and cannot do. Direct answer “Shadow AI” refers to uses of AI tools that are neither validated nor supervised, with in particular the risk of entering sensitive information into public chatbots. A usage charter serves to frame these practices rather than ban them: it says which data must never be entered, which tools are authorised and who checks. It replaces neither the consultation of the works council nor the risk assessment. Présanse, awareness material, 2025 (in French) ↗ · Guide to AI in occupational health services, Shadow AI 01 / Spot Three signs that uses already exist. The risk exists before any official deployment. Weak signal “I just wanted to save five minutes.” A vague instruction, a personal tool and a copy-and-paste are enough for sensitive data to leave the intended framework. Guide to AI in occupational health services → Warning sign Undeclared AI uses. They are among the warning signs of the axis on social relations at work, along with a decline in informal exchanges. The same axis describes divides between advanced users and non-users. AI and psychosocial risks → Rule No sensitive data in a non-validated tool. The Présanse charter prohibits entering personal, medical or identifying data into a tool that the service has not validated. Présanse charter, 2025 (in French) ↗ 02 / Act Four actions, in this order. Understand before setting rules, then give an alternative. Action What it makes possible Map existing uses Understand practices, needs and workarounds without driving them underground. Set out the prohibited data Give concrete examples for personal and health data. Offer authorised solutions Meet real needs with a framework that teams can use. Train and document incidents Improve the framework from the errors and difficulties observed. Présanse, awareness material, 2025 (in French) ↗ 03 / Draft What should an AI charter contain? The ten principles of the Présanse charter, turned into decisions. Written for French occupational health services, it presents itself as an adaptable template. Principle Decision to write down Human oversight Identify who checks, validates, challenges a","url":"en/recommendations/ai-charter/"},{"title":"AI at work: pointers and warning signs for employees","description":"You use an AI tool, an AI organises or assesses your work, or you validate what it produces: the questions to ask, your means of recourse and the warning signs.","keywords":"Recommendations / Employees Recommendations · Employee AI at work: pointers for employees This flowchart applies whether you use an AI tool, have your work organised or assessed by a system, or approve what such a system produces. Several branches may apply to you; they all lead to the warning signs. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the pathway ↓ Open the flowchart → The essentials Essential guidance The principle “AI can affect you even if you do not use it yourself.” The pathway The prevention pathway Start What situation are you in? Choose a branch, or go through them all. A · I use an AI tool for my work Drafting, summarising, searching, coding B · An AI organises, assesses or decides about my work Scheduling, task allocation, scores, activity monitoring C · I approve or supervise what an AI produces Approvals one after another, agents that act D · For everyone: spotting the warning signs And knowing who to talk to Branch A I use an AI tool for my work A1 Is the tool authorised by your employer for this use? If no Do not enter any personal, health, confidential or identifying data. Instead, explain your need to your manager to obtain an authorised solution. Source: AI in OHS services · shadow AI A2 Are you going to enter personal or health data, or data covered by confidentiality? If yes First check where the data goes, how long it is kept and whether it is used for training; health data must be stored with a certified health-data host (HDS). Share only what is strictly necessary, with nothing identifying. Source: Understanding AI · from model to system A3 Can you check the result yourself: skills, sources, time? If no Do not use the result as it is: ask for a competent review or keep the task out of the tool. Producing a result with AI does not mean being able to judge its quality. Source: Reading · Occupational boundaries If yes Check references, figures and legal articles, which may be invented. Be wary of an answer that confirms your hypothesis too quickly, and of instructions hidden in a document the tool reads. Source: Understanding AI · capabilities and limits A4 Does checking take unplanned time, or have your targets increased with AI? If yes Report it to your manager: checking and correcting are part of the work and must be counted. How the time saved is used should be decided collectively, before it is turned into targets. Source: Psychosocial risks · intensity A5 Do you still regularly practise the key skills of your job without AI? If no Keep doing some tasks without AI assistance, especial","url":"en/recommendations/employee/"},{"title":"Deploying AI in a company: five steps for employers and HR","description":"Five gates before deploying AI in a team: demonstrated need, qualified use, organised dialogue, reversible pilot, documented follow-up. Pointers for employers and HR.","keywords":"Recommendations / Employer, management and HR Recommendations · Employer, management or HR Deploying AI in a team: five steps for the employer An AI project passes through five gates before it is deployed, then it is monitored over time. A closed gate calls for the actions shown before moving on to the next one. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the pathway ↓ Open the flowchart → The essentials Essential guidance The principle “Bringing AI into a team is an organisational decision, not just a choice of tool.” To do whatever the situation: For all uses. Map the data: purpose, legal basis, flows, access, processors, retention period. Check whether a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) is required: AI does not automatically call for one, the risks of the processing decide. In all cases. Involve the employees concerned in choosing and configuring the tool, and call on the occupational physician or the multidisciplinary team of the occupational health service while the project can still change. Follow-up schedule. Record the effects at T1 (4 to 8 weeks), T2 (3 months), T3 (6 months), then every 6 to 12 months, and after any change of model, data, objective or population, or after an incident. Indicators remain collective: never used to assess or rank people. The pathway The prevention pathway AI project in a team Gate 1 Proven need 1.1 Does the project start from a specific work problem, with alternatives examined? If no Describe the task, the people concerned, the expected result and the points where an error would have consequences. “Saving time” is not a task. Name who will be able to launch, change or stop the pilot. Sources: Law and governance · Understanding AI · five questions Gate 2 Clearly defined use 2.1 What will the system do? Obligations depend on the function the system performs, not on the software brand. Drafting, summarising, searching Usage note, data entry rules, human verification protocol and review time built into the workload. Recruiting, assessing, managing mobility Sensitive use: AI Act classification (some employment uses are high-risk, with rules applying from 2 December 2027), non-discrimination, informing the works council and candidates in advance, a way to challenge decisions. Labour Code L. 2312-38 · L. 1132-1 Planning or allocating tasks Mapping of work before and after, rules for contesting, attention to autonomy, fairness and work intensification. Monitoring activity or performance Purpose, necessity and proportionality test, informing employees in advance, consulting the ","url":"en/recommendations/employer/"},{"title":"AI and the occupational physician: pointers for practice","description":"Pointers for occupational physicians and nurses: supporting a company that deploys AI, spotting its effects during consultations, using AI in your own service.","keywords":"Recommendations / Occupational physician and nurse Recommendations · Occupational physician or nurse AI: pointers for the occupational physician and the occupational health nurse Two branches, reflecting the twofold goal the site draws from France’s fifth Occupational Health Plan (PST 5): supporting the companies and employees you look after, and using AI cautiously in your own service. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the pathway ↓ Open the flowchart → The essentials Essential guidance The principle “AI concerns both the workers you support and your own practice.” To do whatever the situation: In all cases. Act on the organisation first: checking time counted in the workload, the right to set aside an AI recommendation, training during working hours, the right to disconnect extended to AI, regular unaided practice, collective indicators. Training employees to manage their stress does not make up for an organisation that exposes them to it. Ongoing. Watch for weak signals: jobs not replaced, fewer junior hires, raised targets, growing supervision, skills practised less often. A test question: would the company still function if its AI system disappeared tomorrow? To measure these trends, the 2025 review proposes recording AI exposure in the databases of inter-company occupational health services (SPSTI). Governance. Name who checks, approves and can suspend the use. Log uses, approvals, errors and incidents, and obtain documented feedback before any wider roll-out. The pathway The prevention pathway Start Your question is about… A · A company or employees you look after Advice, medical appointments, workplace action B · The use of AI in your own service Documentation, prevention, member companies, medical practice Branch A Supporting companies and employees A1 Is a company you look after introducing an AI that changes the organisation of work, workload, autonomy or work pace? If yes Offer to contribute before the works council (CSE) gives its opinion: the occupational physician advises employee representatives. Fill in the assessment grid in a meeting, without any individual health data, and analyse concrete work situations. Labour Code R. 4623-1 · L. 2314-3 Sources: Works council and AI · occupational health · Assess an AI project A2 During a medical appointment or interview, do you notice an unusual workload, boredom, anxiety or sleep problems? If yes Explore recent changes in the work, AI included, without automatically attributing these signs to it. Who approves what, and how many times a day? What has","url":"en/recommendations/occupational-physician/"},{"title":"AI and prevention: pointers for OSH specialists and ergonomists","description":"Observe real work, read it through Gollac’s six axes, then propose and follow up measures: pointers for prevention specialists and ergonomists facing an AI project.","keywords":"Recommendations / Prevention specialist and ergonomist Recommendations · Prevention specialist or ergonomist AI at work: pointers for prevention specialists and ergonomists Observe real work, read it through the six Gollac dimensions, then propose and follow up measures. These pointers enrich field observation and discussion about work; they do not replace them. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the pathway ↓ Open the flowchart → The essentials Essential guidance The principle “Automating a task does not necessarily remove the workload.” To do whatever the situation: Prioritise the measures. Act at the source first, on the organisation and on the choice and configuration of the tool; then detect deterioration early; then support the people affected. Training employees to manage their stress does not make up for an organisation that exposes them to it. The pathway The prevention pathway An AI changes a work situation Step 1 Observe real work 1.1 Have you described the work before and after AI, using concrete situations? If no Start from real situations: tasks that have moved, checks, corrections, exceptions, decisions that are influenced. Activity often shifts towards checking outputs and handling the most complex cases. Sources: Homepage · prevention specialist profile · Law and governance · role of prevention 1.2 Do employees approve an agent’s outputs or actions one after another? If yes Analyse supervision as a vigilance task: approvals per hour, time and authority to refuse, session length, breaks, rotation, responsibilities set out in writing. Record it in the DUERP (the company’s occupational risk assessment document) as a situation exposing workers to psychosocial risks. Source: Reading · Human in the loop 1.3 Are tasks from other occupations entering the job thanks to AI? If yes Document what enters and leaves the occupation, the time actually saved, the training needed and who is responsible for checking. Being able to do a task with AI does not automatically mean having the skill. Source: Reading · Occupational boundaries Step 2 Read through the six Gollac dimensions 2.1 On which dimension do the warning signs appear? Each card gives the question to ask for the DUERP and a measure at the source, taken from the prevention fact sheets. 1 · Work intensity and working time Warning signs: raised targets, evening work, cognitive fatigue, training outside working hours. Question for the risk assessment (DUERP): “Has the time saved through AI been reallocated? To what, and who decided?” At the source: mea","url":"en/recommendations/prevention-specialist/"},{"title":"Studying AI at work: methodological pointers for research","description":"Separate a model’s performance from its effects on work, build a human reference, situate each claim: methodological pointers for studying AI at work.","keywords":"Recommendations / Research Recommendations · Researcher Studying AI at work: methodological pointers for research Tell a technical promise apart from an observed benefit, and make methods and their limits explicit. Two research settings: AI in professional practice, and the work of the teams who develop and evaluate the models. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the pathway ↓ Open the flowchart → The essentials Essential guidance The principle “An AI system’s performance is not enough to understand its effects on work and health.” To do whatever the situation: In all cases. Study the organisation (deadlines, staffing, priorities) rather than employees as a “human factor” to be monitored: it is the organisation that sets the conditions for control. Grade each claim. Established, emerging or unknown. Evidence that is still limited is neither proof of inevitable harm nor proof that there is no risk. The pathway The prevention pathway Start Your research is about… Branches A and B lead to C, which is common to all studies. A · The usefulness or effects of AI in professional practice Evaluating a tool, a use, a deployment B · The teams who develop, evaluate or supervise the models Working conditions and AI safety C · For all studies: concluding and publishing Levels of evidence, limits, causality Branch A Evaluating AI in professional practice A1 Does your corpus contain health data or data that could identify someone? If yes De-identify data before anything is sent to a model, and work in an authorised environment (for health data, certified health-data hosting, HDS). To test an online tool, use fictitious texts, like the 540 workplace recommendations in the Préconisations test bench. Sources: Publication · AI and medical recommendations · Préconisations tool (in French) A2 Are you measuring the model’s performance, or its effects on work? A good technical score does not, on its own, predict good workplace use. Model performance Test on cases representative of the real task: frequent errors and serious errors, stability from one answer to the next, resistance to misleading inputs, cost and turnaround time. Effects on work Evaluate the work system: time saved including review, final quality, exceptions, responsibilities, workload, autonomy, skills and cooperation, before and after implementation. Source: Understanding AI · back to real work A3 Are you relying on a published benchmark? If yes Check that it resembles the target work; describe the instructions, tools, number of attempts and version; specify what the","url":"en/recommendations/researcher/"},{"title":"AI and psychosocial risks at work | Prevention 2026","description":"How is AI transforming psychosocial risks at work? The six Gollac dimensions, warning signs, three levels of prevention and the French legal framework.","keywords":"Home Risks and prevention Psychosocial risks and AI Psychosocial risks · Artificial intelligence · Prevention AI and psychosocial risks at work Written by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician, AI lead at the French Society of Occupational Health · Updated on 13 November 2026 By changing tasks, deadlines, control and responsibilities, AI can weaken workers’ resources. This page covers the mechanisms, warning signs and avenues for prevention. Read the essentials ↓ Explore the six dimensions → Direct answer The essentials Artificial intelligence rarely creates an entirely new risk: it reshapes known psychosocial risk factors when it brings lasting changes to work organisation, whether through workload, autonomy, responsibilities, relationships, skills or job security. The available studies do not show a single, uniform effect: some uses improve productivity or control over work, while others are associated with greater surveillance, invisible work, loneliness or exhaustion. Prevention must therefore examine the whole work system, with the people concerned, using the six dimensions of the Gollac report, and then monitor actual effects over time. 01 The risk is not in the model alone. It arises from the interaction between a technical capability, a task, targets, people and an organisation. 02 Findings vary. The cited studies include 5,172 support agents, 197 Australian workers and randomised trials in healthcare. 03 Prevention remains collective. It describes work before and after deployment, monitors warning signs across the six Gollac dimensions, and acts at three levels: at the source, through early detection, and through support. 01 / Six Gollac dimensions How does AI change the six psychosocial risk factors? An AI tool rarely creates an entirely new risk: it reshapes known factors. The six dimensions of the Gollac report describe work organisation and employment, not workers’ presumed vulnerability, and a single deployment can change several dimensions at once. G01 Work intensity and working time Time saved turns into targets; checking time goes uncounted. G02 Emotional demands AI handles simple cases; people inherit the difficult ones. G03 Autonomy The algorithm directs and evaluates work, reducing discretion. AI at work reshapes psychosocial risk factors G04 Social relationships at work Opaque evaluation, less mutual support and recognition. G05 Value conflicts Barriers to quality work, unclear responsibility. G06 Job and work situation insecurity Fear of replacement, poorly explained changes. G01 Intensity and time G02 Emotional demands G03 Au","url":"en/risks-prevention/"},{"title":"AI risks at work: two scales of analysis","description":"Two complementary analyses of AI-related risks: real work and health, followed by the economy and society.","keywords":"Risks · Prevention AI risks at work can be understood at two scales. What AI is changing in work and health today, and what its widespread adoption could change in employment and society tomorrow. Choose an analysis 01 · Actual work and health Read first Psychosocial risks and health What does AI change in work organisation? To analyse a tool, a team or an occupation. Analyse the effects on work → 02 · Economy and society Future scenario Economic and social risk What role does human work retain in creating value? To examine the effects of widespread adoption. Explore the economic and social scenario → Do you have a specific AI project? Assess it in ten minutes, based on actual work. Open the assessment tool → Next steps From analysis to tools. Six pages for moving from an identified risk to a decision. Assessment grid Eighteen questions on nine axes, before the pilot and at follow-up. Prevention fact sheets Seven sheets to download, one per Gollac dimension. French CSE and AI Ten questions before the opinion, and the court decisions. Court decisions, 2025–2026 Each decision with its date, court, outcome and source. Recommendations by audience A pathway for each role, from employer to employee. Law and governance AI Act, GDPR, works council and risk assessment.","url":"en/risks/"},{"title":"AI and jobs: when human work becomes less essential","description":"A forward-looking scenario drawing on The Intelligence Curse: how AI could reduce the economic place of human work, and the signals occupational health can track.","keywords":"← Collective risks HUMAN DISEMPOWERMENT · ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL RISK AI and jobs: when human work gradually ceases to be essential The risk is not limited to the number of jobs lost. It also concerns the place that human work gradually retains in the creation of value, learning and economic power. Precaution This course examines a possible systemic risk. It does not describe a demonstrated consequence of current AI. Read the article The Intelligence Curse ↗ 01 · Assist 02 · Recommend 03 · Decide by default 04 · Act autonomously In brief What this scenario says, and what it does not. The economic risk of AI is not measured only in jobs lost. It lies in the place human work keeps in the creation of value. A gradual shift. Assist, recommend, decide by default, act autonomously: one task, one occupation and one decision at a time. Three possible consequences. Less bargaining power, the loss of the first rungs of careers, and a concentration of economic power. Signals visible early. Posts left unfilled, fewer junior hires, raised targets, more checking and correction work. A scenario, not a finding. It examines a possible systemic risk, not a demonstrated consequence of today’s AI. STARTING POINT The economic risk of artificial intelligence is not just a question: how many jobs will disappear? The change could be much more gradual. As models become capable of performing more intellectual tasks, using software, making decisions and acting autonomously, companies can gradually replace some human labour with another resource: capital, computation and AI models . The hypothesis developed in The Intelligence Curse is simple: if intelligence becomes a resource that can be purchased, copied and deployed almost at will, capital gradually becomes a substitute for labour. Organisations with sufficient resources can then directly purchase the capabilities they need instead of recruiting, training and retaining people. This shift does not require AI to suddenly replace all workers. It can happen one task, one job, and one decision at a time. 01 From copilot to economic infrastructure At first, AI is introduced as a productivity tool. It writes a document, analyses data, helps with programming, prepares a presentation or proposes a decision. Then its role can gradually change: assist → recommend → decide by default → act autonomously As systems become more reliable, human intervention can appear as an additional step: slower, more expensive and sometimes less efficient. A company that automates more can produce faster and at lower cost. Its competitors are then encouraged t","url":"en/risks/economic-social/"},{"title":"AI and psychosocial risks: seven prevention fact sheets (PDF)","description":"Seven downloadable sheets on AI and psychosocial risks: a cover and six Gollac dimensions, with warning signs, prevention measures and French legal references.","keywords":"Tools / Prevention fact sheets Tools / Psychosocial risk prevention Prevention fact sheets: psychosocial risks and AI Seven downloadable sheets to identify and prevent psychosocial risks when AI enters the workplace, based on the six dimensions of the Gollac report. For prevention professionals, the CSE and occupational health services Prevention fact sheets For each of the six Gollac dimensions: what AI changes, warning signs, a question for the occupational risk assessment (DUERP), and three levels of prevention. The cover brings together the legal references for each dimension. Download the series PDF · 7 A4 pages Cover AI at work through the six Gollac dimensions Sheet 1 Work intensity and working time Sheet 2 Emotional demands Sheet 3 Autonomy Sheet 4 Social relationships at work Sheet 5 Value conflicts Sheet 6 Job and work situation insecurity What the sheets contain What each sheet covers. One Gollac dimension per sheet. The detail, with sources, is in the online analysis. Sheet What AI changes Warning signs Question for the risk assessment Sheet 1 Work intensity and working time The volume and complexity of work, time pressure, working hours and schedules, and work–life balance. Rebound effect · Invisible checking · Work intensification · Acceleration · Spillover Read the analysis of this dimension Targets raised after deployment Overtime and evening work Errors and cognitive fatigue Training outside working hours “Has the time saved through AI been reallocated? To what, and who decided?” Sheet 2 Emotional demands Contact with the public and with suffering, the requirement to control or hide emotions, and fear at work. Concentration of difficult cases · Cascading frustration · Justifying the unjustifiable · Emotions under surveillance · Fear Read the analysis of this dimension Increasing incivility Exhaustion and cynicism Requests for transfers More frequent tense interactions “What proportion of the interactions still handled by people is now tense or distressing?” Sheet 3 Autonomy Discretion, participation in decisions, and opportunities to use and develop skills. Algorithmic management · More prescriptive work · Automation bias · Loss of skills · Detailed tracking Read the analysis of this dimension Feeling that one carries out tasks without making decisions Recommendations followed automatically Work blocked by system failures Loss of interest in the profession “Who can reject the tool’s recommendation, and what happens when they do?” Sheet 4 Social relationships at work Relationships with colleagues and managers, support, recognition, fairne","url":"en/tools/prevention-fact-sheets/"},{"title":"Understanding AI at Work | LLMs, Limits, Risks and AGI","description":"A clear introduction to AI systems and large language models: how they work, training, capabilities, limits, risks, benchmarks and the goal of AGI.","keywords":"Understand · Essential concepts Understanding artificial intelligence at work. Understand how AI systems work, what they can do and where their limits lie, to better assess their uses at work. Start reading ↓ Choose a resource Updated in November 2026 Contents 11 chapters, to read in order or on their own. How the models work 01 Identify the system 1 min 02 Inside an LLM 2 min 03 Training 4 min 04 From model to system 3 min What they can do 05 Capabilities and limits 3 min 06 Reading benchmarks 3 min 07 Tracking progress 5 min Risks and use at work 08 System risks 2 min 09 The goal of AGI 4 min 10 Back to real work 1 min 11 Before workplace use 1 min Further reading : resources chosen according to the question you are asking. 01 / Identify the system First, what kind of artificial intelligence are we talking about? The term “AI” covers systems that do not produce the same kind of result. To understand a workplace use, start by identifying what the tool actually does. 01 · Produce Generative AI It creates or transforms text, images, sound or code from an instruction. A conversational assistant based on an LLM belongs to this family. Output New content Watch for Accuracy, sources, data entered 02 · Estimate Predictive AI It estimates a probability, assigns a score or classifies a case based on past data: default risk, case priority or diagnostic support. Output A score or category Watch for Bias, thresholds, false positives and false negatives 03 · Act Agentic AI It pursues a goal by choosing its steps, using tools and adapting its actions to the results obtained. It may rely on an LLM. Output A sequence of executed actions Watch for Action permissions, oversight, traceability 04 · Organise Algorithmic management It allocates tasks, sets processing priorities, measures activity or influences a management decision. It may or may not incorporate generative AI. Output An instruction or decision Watch for Autonomy, avenues of appeal, work intensification Figure 1 · Diagram LLMs: one family among others. Artificial intelligence Solving tasks with computational methods. Example: a rule-based expert system (“if… then…”) Machine learning Learning patterns from data. Example: a filter that sorts emails into spam or not Deep learning Learning with deep neural networks. Several families, depending on what they process LLM · language Drafts a summary of the documents it is given. Vision Spots a defect in a photo, analyses an X-ray. Speech Transcribes an interview, reads a text aloud. Image generation Creates an image or a video from a sentence. Inclusion diagram, not ","url":"en/understand/"},{"title":"Agentic AI: definition, how it works and control points","description":"An AI agent pursues a goal by chaining actions with tools. Definition, how it works, what is measured in November 2026 and the controls to plan at work.","keywords":"Understanding AI at work / Short page Understand · Short page Agentic AI: what are we talking about? What an agent can do, how to define its autonomy and which controls to put in place before assigning it a task. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the guide chapter → Guide contents → The essentials An agent pursues a goal by acting. The definition, then what changes over time. Direct answer Agentic AI pursues a goal by choosing steps, using tools and adapting its actions to the results obtained. It can be built on an LLM. An agent plans, acts, observes the result and starts again, for hours and, according to the providers of the most recent models, sometimes days, with intermittent human supervision. Guide, chapter 01 · Guide, chapter 04 01 / Where it sits What sets an agent apart from other AI systems. Four functions, four outputs, four points of attention. Family What it produces Point of attention Generative AI New content: text, image, sound or code. Accuracy, sources, data entered. Predictive AI A score or a category. Bias, threshold, false positives and false negatives. Agentic AI A sequence of executed actions. Action rights, supervision, traceability. Algorithmic management An instruction or a decision. Autonomy, appeal, intensification. 02 / How it works Tools, a loop, sometimes a team of agents. The model no longer only proposes: it acts. Tools Actions in other software. Web search, code execution, email, calendar or records: the model calls tools through connectors, for example those of the MCP protocol. Some operate a computer directly, screen, keyboard and mouse, as a user would. Loop Act, observe, start again. The agent plans, acts, observes the result and starts again. An error made at the start can spread through the whole chain of actions. Multi-agent A coordinator and specialised agents. One agent can direct others: it splits the goal, hands each part to a specialised agent, then assembles the results. Agents built on the same model share the same blind spots. Anthropic, 2025 ↗ Provider figures · November 2026 Anthropic states that Claude Fable 5.2 can coordinate hundreds of sub-agents, Google offers a “swarm” mode with Gemini 4 Carbon, and OpenAI says it used about 10,000 agents for 88 hours for a mathematical proof that has not been independently verified. These figures say what is possible, not what is reliable. 03 / Measurements What is measured of their autonomy. Two dated reference points, with their limits. Reference What it measures Result Limit Time horizon · METR The length, in wo","url":"en/understand/agentic-ai/"},{"title":"AGI: definitions, measurements and what it means for work","description":"Artificial general intelligence (AGI) has neither a shared definition nor an arrival test. Three ways to define it, what the measurements say and what is at stake for work.","keywords":"Understanding AI at work / Short page Understand · Short page AGI: what are we talking about? Compare definitions of AGI, distinguish measurements from announcements and understand what this debate means for work. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the guide chapter → Guide contents → The essentials A stated goal, a disputed notion. What can be said without betting on a date. Direct answer Artificial general intelligence, or AGI, is a goal stated by several laboratories. There is neither a shared definition nor a test that would establish its arrival. Announcements are not measurements: measured, dated indicators are more informative than statements from players engaged in a race for funding. Guide, chapter 09 01 / Definitions Three ways of defining it. Each sheds light on what is at stake for work. By work Outperforming humans at most jobs. OpenAI’s charter (2018) aims at “highly autonomous systems” that outperform humans at most economically valuable work. The definition is explicitly economic: it is measured by the substitution of human work. OpenAI Charter, 2018 ↗ By levels A progression rather than a threshold. Google DeepMind crosses the level of performance, from “emerging” to “superhuman”, with the breadth of tasks covered. The degree of autonomy left to the system is treated separately: it is a deployment choice, not only a capability. Morris et al., 2023 ↗ By cognition Comparing with the abilities of an educated adult. A group of researchers measures models on ten broad abilities of human cognition. GPT-4 scored 27% and GPT-5 57%, with a very uneven profile: extensive knowledge, almost no long-term memory. Hendrycks, Bengio et al., 2025 ↗ 02 / Dated reference points What the measurements say, and the announcements. Four reference points, to read with their source. Reference What it indicates Source Cognitive profile Across ten abilities each scored out of 10, GPT-5 reaches 57%. Long-term memory storage remains at zero: the model learns nothing lasting from one conversation to the next, unless the tool adds an external memory. As of 13 November 2026, no more recent model has been assessed with this grid. Hendrycks, Bengio et al., 2025 ↗ Levels of AGI In 2023, the authors classed general AI at the “emerging” level; the “competent” level had not been reached. More recent models have not been officially reclassified. Morris et al., 2023 ↗ Announcements On 3 September 2026, OpenAI’s president said he personally considered AGI to have been reached; nine days later, the head of Anthropic called for the pa","url":"en/understand/agi/"},{"title":"AI benchmarks: how to read a model leaderboard","description":"There is no universal ranking of the best AI model. Five families of tests, their limits and four questions to ask before drawing a conclusion for professional use.","keywords":"Understanding AI at work / Short page Understand · Short page AI benchmarks: how to read a model leaderboard Understand what a test measures, its limits and what to check before using a leaderboard to choose a model. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the guide chapter → Guide contents → The essentials There is no universal leaderboard. A score describes a test, not your work. Direct answer There is no universal ranking of the “best model”. A benchmark measures performance on a set of tasks, with a given prompt, metric and conditions. Change one of these and the ranking can change. None of these tests says which model suits your use: that is checked on your own tasks. Guide, chapter 06 01 / Families of tests Five families of tests, five limits. Knowing what a test measures also means knowing what it leaves out. Family Principle and examples Main limit Knowledge Multiple-choice questions, marked automatically. Examples: MMLU, GPQA. Highly exposed to contamination: the questions end up in the training data. Real tasks The model must produce a verifiable result: fix a program, deliver a project. Examples: SWE-bench, Remote Labor Index. Costly to build and to score; they cover only part of a job. Human preference Two anonymous answers, one person votes. Example: LMArena. Measures appeal as much as accuracy. Autonomous behaviour The model acts over time, with no expected answer at each step. Examples: METR’s time horizon, Vending-Bench. No single “right answer”; the result depends heavily on the test set-up. Occupation A test built by professionals in the field. Examples: MedHELM in medicine; BenchMedTrav in occupational health, designed by the author of this site. Closest to the job, but real deployment still has to be assessed. 02 / Dated snapshot The leader changes with the test. As of 13 November 2026, and soon out of date: follow the links for current results. Test What it measures Leader Capabilities Index (ECI) ↗ · Epoch AI An aggregate of several dozen tests. Claude Fable 5.2 GDPval-AA ↗ · Artificial Analysis Professional tasks, compared in pairs. Claude Opus 5.5 ARC-AGI-3 ↗ · ARC Prize Adaptation to novel environments, under the standard protocol. Claude Fable 5.2, with 81.2% Two precautions The gaps between the leaders are often smaller than the measurement uncertainty. And the score depends on the protocol: on ARC-AGI-3, with each provider’s own set-up, four models exceed 97%. 03 / Method Four questions before drawing a conclusion. To ask of any score, whatever the test. 01 · Task Does the test resembl","url":"en/understand/ai-benchmarks/"},{"title":"AI hallucinations: why language models get things wrong","description":"Invented references, sycophancy, unfaithful displayed reasoning: six persistent errors of language models, where they come from and the checks to plan at work.","keywords":"Understanding AI at work / Short page Understand · Short page AI hallucinations: why models get things wrong Why models produce errors, what studies can tell us about them and where to place the checks. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the guide chapter → Guide contents → The essentials A plausible answer is not a true answer. What the word refers to, and why the error cannot be fixed with a setting. Direct answer A hallucination, or confabulation, is a plausible but false answer: an invented reference, legal article or figure, presented with confidence. The model produces what is probable in the context, not what is true. These limits are not simple tuning defects. They follow from the way models are trained, evaluated and built into tools. Kalai et al., OpenAI, 2025 ↗ · Guide, chapter 05 01 / Six errors Six errors that persist. Knowing them helps place the checks in the right spot. Error Where it comes from At work Confabulation Pre-training does not teach the model to tell a rare fact from a plausible invention, and evaluations that score only accuracy reward guessing over abstaining. Kalai et al., 2025 ↗ Invented references, legal articles or figures, presented with confidence. Sycophancy Tuning on human preferences can favour approval at the expense of accuracy. In April 2025, OpenAI withdrew a GPT-4o update judged excessively flattering. OpenAI, 2025 ↗ The tool tends to confirm the hypothesis contained in the question asked. Jagged frontier Capabilities do not follow the difficulty a human perceives: excellent here, failing right next door. Dell’Acqua et al., 2023 ↗ Trust earned on one task does not transfer to the neighbouring task. Displayed reasoning The reasoning a model displays does not faithfully reflect what determined its answer: in an Anthropic study, the models tested reported a hint they had used in only 25 to 39% of cases. Chen et al., 2025 ↗ A convincing justification does not prove that the answer is well founded. Prompt injection The model does not reliably separate the user’s instructions from the text it reads: a web page, an email or a PDF can contain hidden instructions. OWASP, LLM01:2025 ↗ The risk rises as soon as the tool can send, modify or delete. Variability Generation is probabilistic and sensitive to wording, to the order of documents and to the model version. Its knowledge stops at a cut-off date, unless it can consult live sources. Test several times, date the trials and record the version used. 02 / Two studies What two studies show. One on consultants, the other in occ","url":"en/understand/ai-hallucinations/"},{"title":"What is an LLM? How a large language model works","description":"An LLM is a neural network trained to predict what comes next in a text. How it produces an answer, how it is trained, and what to check before using it at work.","keywords":"Understanding AI at work / Short page Understand · Short page What is an LLM? How a language model produces an answer, how it is trained and what to check before relying on it. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Updated on 13 November 2026 Read the guide chapter → Guide contents → The essentials An LLM predicts what comes next in a text. Direct answer A large language model, or LLM, is a neural network trained to predict what comes next in a text. It does not look up a ready-made answer in a database: it composes one, unit by unit. It computes probabilities from the context; it does not spontaneously check whether its answer is true or compliant. CNIL, definition of a language model (in French) ↗ · Guide, chapter 02 01 / How it works Five steps between your question and the answer. The same computation starts again for every unit of text produced. Split: the text becomes tokens. The prompt and the documents supplied are cut into small units: words, pieces of words or signs. Represent: tokens become numbers. Each token is converted into a numerical representation that encodes the regularities learned during training. Relate: attention weighs the context. The Transformer examines the relations between tokens to determine which parts of the context matter most at that moment. Vaswani et al., 2017 ↗ Predict: the next token is chosen. The model computes a probability distribution. Generation settings influence the choice and how much it varies. Repeat: the answer is built step by step. The chosen token joins the context, then the computation starts again until the answer ends. Illustrative example After “The employee describes persistent pain in the…”, the model assigns a probability to each possible continuation: “back”, “wrist”, “knee”. It chooses what is probable in that context, not what is true for that employee. In the guide, the probabilities in this example are fictitious. 02 / How it is built Five training stages, and the flaw each can leave behind. An LLM’s capabilities are not programmed rule by rule. Stage What comes out Inherited flaw Pre-train on web text, books and code A base model, which completes text without following instructions. Confabulation, biases and errors from the source texts; knowledge frozen at a cut-off date. Fine-tune on exemplary dialogues An assistant, which answers requests and refuses some of them. It takes on the preferences of those who write and validate the examples. Reinforce from compared answers An assistant that is more useful and more cautious in its answers. Sycophancy: what pleases can win over wha","url":"en/understand/llm/"},{"title":"AI in occupational health services: uses, risks, method","description":"Sourced guide on AI in occupational health services: real use cases, the Présanse charter, health data, the AI Act, risks and a method for launching a pilot.","keywords":"Home AI in OHS services Practical guide · OHS services · Sources 2025–2026 AI in occupational health services: uses, risks and deployment method. Author: Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician, AI lead of the SFST · Updated on September 11, 2026 In an occupational health service, artificial intelligence can assist with documentation, administration, prevention, member relations and certain strictly supervised medical uses. It replaces neither the analysis of work nor professional decision-making. Explore uses ↓ Test the matrix PST 5 · 2026–2030 What does PST 5 provide for AI at work? The national plan makes AI at work a subject for prevention. In Objective 3, the Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030 requires AI-related transformations to be developed in consultation, respect working conditions and be evaluated through their real effects. Sub-action 3.1.1 provides for the creation of a national observatory for AI at work, led by the National Agency for the Improvement of Working Conditions (Anact). 01 Study both sides. PST 5 aims to document AI both as a tool for prevention and for improving working conditions, and as a possible source of occupational risks through AI systems and their uses. 02 Produce useful knowledge. The plan provides for quantitative and qualitative studies, experiments, training, webinars as well as tools and methods to move from findings to action. 03 Give OHS services an explicit role. Appendix 3 describes prevention and occupational health services as “pivotal players”. It asks them to contribute to knowledge about AI with Présanse and Anact, while developing a considered use of AI in their own activities. For an occupational health service, the goal is therefore twofold: cautiously test well-defined professional uses and observe what AI deployment changes in the companies it supports — workload, autonomy, skills, work groups and occupational risks. Source: Occupational health plan 2026–2030, action 3.1.1, p. 44–45, and annex 3, p. 154–156. Consult the official document ↗ 01 / What uses of AI in an OHS service? Five families. Different levels of evidence and risk. Présanse groups the cases reported in occupational health services into five families. This classification describes a field of uses; it does not demonstrate the effectiveness of each tool. Présanse presentation ↗ 01 · Medical practices Assist, never conclude. Transcription, synthesis, documentary research or second reading: the level of vigilance increases as soon as health data or an individual decision is concerned. Uses cited by Présanse ↗ 02 · Prevention Structuri","url":"en/uses-and-field/occupational-health-example/"}];
